## 1. Introduction
Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) are widely used in power transportation/storage for portable devices for their advantages of high energy/power density and environmental friendliness [1,2]. Considering the theoretic capacity limit of transition metal oxides/carbon electrode system in commercial LIBs, intense efforts on developing new electrode materials are explored to promote the overall performance potential of LIBs [3-6].
Silicon is a promising candidate for LIBs due to its high theoretical specific capacity $(4200\mathrm{mAh/g})$ , low voltage platform (lower than 0.4V), and abundant sources [7]. However, its severe volumetric expansion $(\approx300\%)$ during electrochemically lithiating process will lead to the pulverization of silicon active center, resulting in unstable solid electrolyte interface (SEI) film, electrical disconnection as well as dramatic capacity fading on cycling [8,9]. Furthermore, its intrinsic semi-conductive property with relatively low electronic conductivity $({\approx}10^{-3}\mathrm{s}/\mathrm{cm})$ and lithium ion diffusion coefficient $({\approx}10^{-14}{\sim}10^{-13}\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{s})$ is another issue to be addressed.
Admirable configuration of nanostructured silicon composite where active or inert hosts with versatile microstructure function effectively as the stress-buffering layer, structure stabilizer as well as electron/ion transferring tunnels is preferred to modify the poor electrochemical performance of silicon [10-17]. Among the conventional modifications, porous structure with versatile spatial configurations is the most favorable architecture for enhancing the performance of silicon due to its attractively extra space for accommodating the volumetric contrast caused by the inserting/extracting behavior of lithium ions into/out of silicon active center [15,16]. In addition, the porous structure could also increase the contact area between the electrode material and the electrolyte to shorten the diffusion path of lithium ions and accelerate the electrode reaction kinetics [17]. 1D-3D carbonaceous or graphite-type materials, such as carbon nanotubes (CNTs), graphene or pyrolyzed polymer-derived carbon are preferentially chosen as porous framework for silicon anode because of their high ionic/electric conductivity, structure-designing flexibility as well as low volumetric expansion $(9\%)$ for lithium intercalation [17-22]. These strategies have been proved to efficiently enhance the kinetics and the cycling stability of silicon. However, the contrasting volumetric expansion rate of silicon $(300\%)$ and carbon $(9\%)$ after lithium ion insertion still induces a risk of possible interface de-bonding and structural collapse on cycling in spite of the flexible carbonaceous framework. Interface bonding enhancement is indispensable to compensate the gap of volumetric changes between silicon and carbonaceous matrix [21]. Flexible polymers might be potential candidates for the modification of silicon due to their intrinsic flexibility and tunable chemical affinity with the surface of silicon. However, further modification is definitely necessary for the lithiating-inert property and low ionic/electric conductivity of many polymers.
It is interesting to note that some metal-organic framework materials (MOFs) composed of metal center and organic ligand, whose structure, conductivity and chemical function could be designed flexibly by changing their metal centers and organic functional groups, have been demonstrated active in electrochemistry circumstances [23-31]. Therefore, in this work, we propose a new strategy of anchoring $\mathrm{Si@C}$ onto petal-like nickel metal-organic frameworks (Ni-MOFs) to configure a porous double-layered Si@C-Ni-MOF composite to achieve a highly stable structure resisting against volumetric expansion and finally enhance the cycle life. Incomplete carbonized carbon layer shelled on silicon not only acts as protective layer for avoiding the direct contact of silicon with electrolytes, but plays an important role in anchoring Si $@\mathrm{C}$ particles onto MOFs via moderate chemical bonding, which can effectively reduce the structural damage and interface-debonding of silicon. The resulting NiMOF-Si $@\mathrm{c}$ composite maintains admirable stability on cycling, and the retention capacity $1545.3\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ could be maintained with a capacity retention rate of $99.79\%$ after 300 cycles at a current density of $200\mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
## 2. Experimental section
2.1 Synthesis of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ composite materials
$0.3\mathrm{g}$ silicon powder (particle size ${<}40\mathrm{nm}$ ) was ultrasonically dispersed in a mixture of $200\mathrm{ml}$ deionized water and $70\mathrm{ml}$ alcohol for $20\ \mathrm{\min}$ . Then $0.25\mathrm{g}$ cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) as a surfactant was added into the solution and stirred for 10 minutes. Subsequently, $0.075\mathrm{g}$ resorcinol, $0.3\mathrm{ml}$ ammonia and $0.1125\mathrm{g}$ formaldehyde were also added, and the mixed solution was stirred at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 20h. The products were centrifuged and then washed alternately with water and ethanol for three times. The collected products were dried in vacuum at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 hours followed by a calcination process at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in argon for 2h to obtain $\mathrm{Si@C}$ composite.
2.2 Synthesis of Si@C-Ni-MOF composites
$0.05\mathrm{g}\mathrm{Si}@\mathrm{C}$ composite was ultrasonically dispersed in $50\mathrm{ml}$ alcohol solution for 15 minutes. 0.225 g $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ (1,3,5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid) and 0.225 g of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was then added into the solution and continued stirring for 10 minutes. The solution was transferred into an $80\mathrm{ml}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and kept at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The obtained precipitates were centrifuged and washed with distilled water three times, followed by drying in a vacuum oven at $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 hours. Si@C-Ni-MOF composite was finally prepared.
## 2.3 Material characterization
The morphology and the microstructure of the prepared materials were analyzed by scanning electron microscope (SEM) (SUPER 55 SAPPHRE), X-ray diffraction (XRD) (RigakuD/MAX-3A) and transmission electron microscope (TEM) (JEM 2100F). The content of Si and C in the prepared materials was investigated using a thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA) (STA409C). The analysis of specific surface area and porosity of the samples was conducted on a Nitrogen physical adsorption and desorption tester (WBL-810). The chemical bonds and functional groups of the materials were analyzed by infrared spectroscopy (Frontier). The valence states of Si, Ni, O and C elements were determined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (KAlpha 1063).
## 2.4 Electrochemical Performance Test
The prepared active materials of Si@C-Ni-MOF, acetylene black and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) were mixed in an N-Methyl pyrrolidone (NMP) solvent with a weight ratio of 7: 2: 1, followed by ball milling for 40 minutes to get the slurry. The slurry was then coated on copper foil and dried in vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 hours to get the electrodes, which was then into $14\mathrm{mm}$ discs and mounted in 2025 coin cell as the working electrode. The load of active substances in the electrode sheet is between $0.35\mathrm{mg}$ and $0.56\mathrm{{mg}}$ . The performance of the electrodes were tested with lithium foil as the counter electrode and 1M $\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}$ / EC (Ethylene carbonate) $+$ DMC (Dimethyl carbonate) (1 ： 1 in volume) as the electrolyte. Galvanostatic charge/discharge measurements were conducted on a battery test system (LANDCT2001A). Cyclic voltammetry (CV) and Galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) tests were performed on an electrochemical workstation (CHI660C).
## 3. Results and discussion
The synthesis process of Si@C-Ni-MOF composite proposed in our work is illustrated in Figure 1. Firstly, $\mathrm{Si@C}$ is synthesized by the pyrolysis process of in-situ synthesized resin on the surface of silicon nanoparticles (designated as $\mathrm{Si}@\mathrm{RF})$ , whose configuration could efficiently prevent silicon active center from the direct contact with electrolytes. It is worth noting that incomplete pyrolysis of the synthesized resin layer coated on the surface of silicon nanoparticles was strategically controlled on the purpose of enhancing the conductivity as well as retaining some active functional groups on carbon chain, which can definitely facilitate the interface bonding between Si $@\mathrm{C}$ and Ni-MOF in the subsequent synthesis. In the second step, petal-like Ni-MOF is in-situ self-assembled by the simple reaction between $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ in $\mathrm{Si@C}$ suspension in alcohol via solvothermal method, during which $\mathrm{Si@C}$ nanoparticles are uniformly anchored onto the petal-like Ni-MOF lamellas to form Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF composite. The resulting composite possesses a strong interface, a large specific surface area and a controllable porous structure, benefiting for structural stability, sufficient contact area with the electrolyte as well as a fast lithium ion channel.
Figure 2a is the XRD pattern of the prepared Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF sample. The characteristic diffraction peaks of crystal silicon at $33.1^{\circ}$ , $55.5^{\circ}$ , $66.2^{\circ}$ and $82.4^{\circ}$ could be found [32], and those peaks in the range of $5^{\circ}-20^{\circ}$ are assigned to the diffraction of Ni-MOF (Figure S1a), coincident with the reported results [33,34]. The diffraction peaks occurring at 9.2, 11.2, 17.4, 20.7 and 26 are corresponded to (100), (010), (200), (101) and (020), respectively (CCDC no. 638866). The morphology and the microstructure of the sample were further clarified by SEM and TEM as shown in Figure 2b-2e. Figure 2b shows the morphology of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ . Interconnected $\mathrm{Si@C}$ particles with relatively smooth surface could be found, which confirms that the surface coating layer composed of the resin-pyrolyzed carbon is uniform and perfectly inherits the morphology of the pristine silicon nanoparticles. Figure 2c-2e present the morphology of the as-synthesized Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF. Si $@\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles are dispersed among the petal-shaped Ni-MOF nanosheets (Figure 2c-2d) [35-37]. The interconnected Ni-MOF nanosheets creates a porous structure, which not only benefits for the efficient transportation of electrolytes, but possibly provides sufficient space for accommodating the volumetric expansion of silicon during lithiation process.
Figure 2. (a) XRD patterns of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ and Si@C-Ni-MOF, (b) SEM image of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ , (c) SEM images of Si@C-Ni-MOF, (d) and (e) TEM and HRTEM images of $\operatorname{Si}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{C-Ni-}$ MOF, (f) SEAD patterns of Si@C-Ni-MOF, (g) Nitrogen adsorption and desorption curves of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ and Si@C-Ni-MOF and pore size distribution of Si $@\mathrm{C}$ and $\operatorname{Si}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{C-Ni-}$ MOF (inset), (h) Thermogravimetric curves of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ and Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF.
HRTEM image of Si@C-Ni-MOF in Figure 2e clarifies that the thickness of the resin-pyrolyzed coating layer on the surface of silicon is about $4{\cdot}5\mathrm{nm}$ . The crystalline interplanar spacing distance of $0.314\mathrm{nm}$ corresponds to the (111) crystal face of crystalline silicon. The crystal lattice fringe of crystalline Ni-centered zone （red dash line circled ） demonstrates the formation of Ni-MOFs around $\mathrm{Si@C}$ . The SAED patterns shown in Figure 2f are corresponding to the (111), (220) and (311) crystal face of Silicon respectively. The crystal lattice fringe of crystalline Ni-MOF is shown in Figure S1b. The average distance between adjacent lattice planes is about $0.24~\mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to (010) plane of Ni-MOF. The results of XRD and TEM analysis show that crystalline Si and Ni-MOF coexist in the Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF composite material. Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ presents that mesoporous structure is involved in the synthesized Si@C-NiMOF according to the typical IV-type isotherm adsorption-desorption curve with hysteresis loop, which is coincident with the porous structure of Ni-MOF (Figure S2). Contrastingly, no pores are included in Si@C. The pore size distribution (inset of Figure 2g) of Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF indicates a large amount of macropores are also formed during the assembling process. Thermogravimetric analysis was conducted to further verify the content of silicon and carbon in the composite. Ni was changed into NiO at high temperature during the TG test, and the silicon in this case would be oxidized into $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ above $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ according to the TG results of pure $\mathrm{Si@{C}}$ , so the residue weight of $\mathrm{Si@C-}$ Ni-MOF after TG test below $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is actually the sum weight of Si and NiO. There is $8.05\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Ni element involved in the final composite according to the element analysis by ICP (Inductive Coupled Plasma Emission Spectrometer). Therefore, the content of silicon could be coarsely calculated as follows: $\mathrm{Siwt^{0}/_{0}+N i w t^{0}/_{0}/W_{N i}{^\ast}W_{N i O}{=}}23\%$ , where $\mathrm{W_{Ni}}$ and ${\sf W}_{\mathrm{NiO}}$ is the molecular weight of Ni and NiO. The calculated of Si content is about $12.75\mathrm{wt\%}$ in the composite. The thermogravimetric curves (Figure 2h) indicate that the content of silicon in $\mathrm{Si@C}$ and Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF is about $80\%$ and $12.75\mathrm{wt\%}$ respectively.
Fourier transform infrared spectrum (FTIR) and XPS were used to identify the chemical and element composition of as-prepared samples (Figure 3). FTIR spectrum of Si@RF, Si $@\mathrm{C}$ and Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF is shown in Figure 3a. The dominant functional groups of the resin chain are kept well by moderate heat-treatment at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ accompanied with the transformation from Si@RF to Si $@\mathrm{C}$ . The C-O vibration in the carboxylic acid functional group is mainly in the range of $1387{\sim}1600\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The peak at $1575\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is caused by $scriptstyle{\mathrm{~}}O={\mathrm{C-O}}$ asymmetric stretching vibration, and the peak at $1365\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ belongs to $scriptstyle{\mathrm{O=C-O}}$ symmetric stretching vibration [38]. The characteristic absorption peak at $765\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is mainly caused by C-H bending vibration. The characteristic absorption peaks at 1100 and $1200~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ correspond to the stretching vibration of the asymmetric Si-O-Si bond and the stretching of the Si-O bond or Si-OH bond respectively, whose intensity is enhanced obviously in $\mathrm{Si@C}$ with comparison to that of $\operatorname{Si}\mathcal{@\mathrm{RF}}$ due to the reaction between silicon surface and O element at high temperature. A weak absorption at $790\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ of Si-C bond in $\mathrm{Si@C}$ sample reveals that chemical bonding with the interaction between silicon active center and the surface resin layer has been taken place unavoidably during heat treatment. The characteristic absorption peak at $726\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ caused by the stretching vibration of Ni-O is found in the sample of Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF.
Figure 3b-3f shows the XPS spectrum of Si@C-Ni-MOF. The survey XPS spectrum (as seen in Figure 3b) reveals that the sample contains four elements of C, O, Ni and Si without other impurities. Figure 3c-3f present the fitting spectra of C1s, O1s, Ni2p and Si2p, respectively. As shown in Figure 3c, three peaks located at $284.4\mathrm{eV}$ , $285.4\mathrm{eV}$ and $289.1\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to C1s of $\mathrm{C-C/C=C}$ , C-O and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ respectively. The peaks of $530.8\mathrm{eV}$ and $532.9\mathrm{eV}$ inO1s are assigned to Ni-O bond and $0.0{=}0$ bond, respectively. The peaks in the Ni2p spectrum centered at $857.6\mathrm{eV}$ and $875.5\mathrm{eV}$ could be assigned to Ni2p3/2 and Ni2p1/2 related to Ni-O bond [39]. The other two secondary peaks $875.5\mathrm{eV}$ and 881.1eV are satellite peaks of $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}\left[40{-}42\right]$ . The Si2p spectrum has three peaks, corresponding to pure silicon $(97.3\mathrm{eV})$ , Si-C $(97.9\mathrm{{eV})}$ and Si-O (101.3eV) [43, 44], coincident with the results of FTIR.
Figure 4a shows the cycle performance of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ , Ni-MOF and Si@C-Ni-MOF electrodes under the current density of $200\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ . The initial capacity of the $\mathrm{Si@C}$ electrode is as high as $2252.4\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ . However, it fades dramatically to $564.4~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles. It is interesting to note that although the reversible capacity of NiMOF electrode is only $985.4~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ with a relatively low coulombic efficiency of $67.6\%$ , it exhibits relatively good cycling stability and the reversible capacity could be remained at $764.6\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ with a capacity retention rate of $78\%$ after 100 cycles (Figure S3). Si@C-Ni-MOF exhibits the most stable cyclability with a capacity retention of $1098.9\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles. The calcined temperature of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ plays an important role in the configuration of the stable structure of Si@C-Ni-MOF. The calcined temperature lowered than $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the preparation of Si@C could lead obvious capacity-drop and unstable cylcability (Figure S4) due to the low carbonized degree and thus low conductivity. Figure S4a and Figure S4b shows the cycling performance of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ calcined at $300\mathrm{~\textdegree~}$ and the corresponding Si@C-Ni-MOF respectively. It is noting that the cycling performance of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ calcined at $300\mathrm{~\textdegree~}$ is unstable (Figure S4a) and very poor, due to the low carbonized degree and thus poor conductivity. Therefore, the electrochemical performance of the prepared Si@C-Ni-MOF shows obvious fading tendency on cycling (Figure S4b). Figure S4c shows the cycle performance of Si $@\mathrm{C}$ calcined at $400\mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{~C~}$ under the current density of $200\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The initial capacity of the $\mathrm{Si@C}$ electrode is as high as 2977 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . However, it fades dramatically to $98.4\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 10 cycles. Figure S4d shows the cycle performance of Si@C-Ni-MOF $(400~\mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{~C~})$ ) electrodes under the current density of $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The cycling stability of Si@C-Ni-MOF electrode is extremely poor and drops obviously after 60 cycles. It is rational to deduce that the combination of moderate residue function groups and the conductivity of carbon coating layer is crucial to configure an admirable interface bonding between $\mathrm{Si@C}$ and Ni-MOF, which plays an important role in combining the advantages of the high capacity of the $\mathrm{Si@C}$ electrode and the stability of the Ni-MOF electrode.
In order to explore the intercalation and deintercalation mechanism of lithium ions in Si@C-Ni-MOF, cyclic voltammetry performance was tested. Figure 4b shows the CV curve of Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF composite material with the voltage window of 0-3V at the scanning speed of $0.1\mathrm{mv}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . There is no obvious SEI film formation process in the first anodic curve of Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF with comparison to the counterpart in the CV curve of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ (Figure S5), which demonstrates that the incorporation of the petal like structure of Ni-MOF suppresses the direct decomposition of the electrolyte on the surface of the electrode to some extent [45]. It also indirectly confirms that the surface of $\mathrm{Si@C}$ is essentially coated with Ni-MOFs to prevent the direct exposure to the electrolyte. $\mathrm{Li}_{22}\mathrm{Si}_{5}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{12}\mathrm{Si}_{7}$ are formed in the lithiating process of silicon, corresponding to the reduction peak existing at 0.2V. Correspondingly, the oxidation peaks at 0.34V and 0.51V are related to the de-lithiating process of LiXSi [46]. A pair of redox peaks at $0.85\mathrm{V}$ and 1.41V (seen in Figure S5b) corresponds to the insertion/extraction of lithium ions into/out of the organic chain of Ni-MOF including benzene ring and carboxylic acid groups [47, 48].
Figure 4c shows the constant current charge-discharge curve of the first cycle under different current densities. The polarization degree of Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF electrode increases with the increase of current density from $100\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ to $2000\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ . As shown in Figure 4d, the Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF electrode has a charge/discharge specific capacity of 1138.1mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}/2291.4\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. With the current density gradually increasing to 200mA $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , 500mA $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , $\mathrm{1000mA~g^{-1}}$ and 2000mA $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the specific discharge capacity of Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF electrode gradually reduces to $1186.5\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , 963.3mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , 889.9 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and $732.5\ \mathrm{\mAh}\ \mathrm{\Omega}_{\mathrm{~\xi~}}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ respectively. When the current density returns to $100\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , the electrode still maintains excellent cycle stability. Figure 4e shows the cycling performance of $\mathrm{Si@C-}$ Ni-MOF electrode at a current density of $200\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The discharge capacity of the Si@C-Ni-MOF electrode is $1548.4\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ in the first cycle. The capacity of the $\mathrm{Si@C^{\underline{{-}}}}$ Ni-MOF electrode shows a tendency to decrease first and then slowly increase on cycling due to the activation of the electrode, which has been widely reported in the research of metal organic framework-based materials [49-51].  After 300 cycles, the capacity is maintained at $1545.3\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ with a Coulombic efficiency of $99.34\%$ and the capacity retention rate of $99.79\%$ . The cycling performance of Si@C-Ni-MOF is dramatically enhanced by the admirable configuration of Ni-MOF framework, showing much higher long-term stability than $\mathrm{Si@C}$ . Figures S6a and Figure S6b shows the cycling performance of Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF electrodes at current densities of $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $2000\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The cycling performance of Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF at $1000\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and $2000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ shows a similar tendency to that at $200\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ which decreases first and then increases on cycling due to the activation of the electrode. However, the capacity at $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ tend to be stable after 200 cycles, while where is about after 500 cycles at higher current density of $2000\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ . This might be ascribed to the hysteresis of ion/electronic diffusion behavior. Further work on reasonable chargedischarge system should be undertaken.
Cyclic voltammetry with different scanning rates was conducted to further determine the diffusion coefficient of lithium ions in Si@C-Ni-MOF (Figure 5a). Figure 5b shows the relationship of the peak current (I) and the square root of the scan rate $\big(\mathrm{\boldmath~V~}^{0.5}\big)$ of the PS/C composite. The ion diffusion rate in the electrode system is calculated by the following formula:
$$
\mathrm{I_{P}}\mathrm{=}(2.69^{\ast}10^{5})\mathrm{n^{1.5}A D^{0.5}C v^{0.5}}
$$
Galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) tests were also performed to calculate the lithium ion chemical diffusion coefficient of Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF electrode with comparison to the results of CV measurement. The GITT procedure consists of a series of current pulses, each followed by a relaxation time. When the current pulse is interrupted, i.e., during the relaxation time, the composition in the electrode tends to become homogeneous by Li-ions diffusion. In order to guarantee accurate results, the relaxation time in our study was set as 9 times of the pulse time. The voltage range of GITT test is $0{\sim}3\mathrm{V}$ , and the current density is 200mAh.The potential-time function curve is shown in Figure 6a. The chemical diffusion coefficient of lithium ions is calculated by the following formula [52]:
$$
D_{{\cal L}i}\mathrm{\Sigma}^{+}=\frac{4}{\pi\tau}\left(\frac{n_{m}V_{m}}{S}\right)^{2}\left(\frac{\Delta E_{s}}{\Delta E_{t}}\right)^{2},
$$
Here, $\tau$ is the duration of the current pulse (s); $\mathfrak{n}_{\mathrm{m}}$ is the number of moles (mol); $\mathrm{V_{m}}$ is the molar volume of the electrode $\left(\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{mol}\right)$ ; S is the contact area of the electrode/electrolyte $(\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ; $\Delta\mathrm{Es}$ is the steady-state voltage change caused by the current pulse and ΔEt is the voltage change during the constant current pulse. Figure 6b is an enlarged view of one GITT cycle in the potential-time function curve. The dotted line indicates the start and end of the discharge pulse. Figure 6c-6d show the lithium ion chemical diffusion coefficients of the Si $@$ C-Ni-MOF electrode during the dischargecharge process, respectively. The average diffusion coefficient of lithium ion in $\mathrm{Si@C^{-}}$ Ni-MOF in our study during charge and discharge is about $5.14901\times10^{-11}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{S}^{-1}$ and $2.57167{\times}10^{-11}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{S}^{-1}$ respectively, superior to the reported results [20,53]. There is a rough agreement with the results of CV test on the order of magnitude of D value. It is worth noting that the diffusion coefficient does not change significantly with the change of voltage, which indicates that the petal-like Ni-MOF structure can improve the kinetics of lithium ion, thereby improving the electrochemical performance of the silicon electrode.
## 4. Conclusions
Si@C-Ni-MOF composite material with strong interface bonding was synthesized by a facile solvothermal method. Partial carbonization of the resin assembled on the surface of silicon nanoparticles was strategically controlled on the purpose of igniting the strong interface bonding between $\mathrm{Si@C}$ and Ni-MOF skeleton. Si@C nanoparticles can thus be uniformly dispersed and anchored on the in-situ assembled 2D petal-shaped Ni-MOFs. The porous structure configured by Ni-MOFs and the strong interface effectively enhances the structural stability and electrochemical kinetics of the silicon composite. Therefore, the Si@C-Ni-MOF composite material presents excellent electrochemical performance. The reversible specific capacity is maintained up to $1545.3\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ with a capacity retention rate of $99.79\%$ after 300 cycles at a current density of $200\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ . Enhanced kinetics has been demonstrated. The tunable configuration and function of MOFs and the delicate control of interface bonding might provide a flexible approach for buffering the volumetric stress of alloytype anode materials for metal ionic batteries.
